<h2>How the course runs</h2>
<p>Each class works through one or two chapters. The exercises at the end of each module are the best practice for
  the quizzes, the assignments, and the exams: try to write each proof yourself before checking it against the notes.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Attendance (10%)</strong> — random attendance checks during the term.</li>
  <li><strong>Programming assignments (30%)</strong> — two assignments, implementing and analyzing algorithms from the course.</li>
  <li><strong>Weekly quizzes (5%)</strong> — a short online quiz each week.</li>
  <li><strong>Midterm exam (25%)</strong> — in week 5, covering weeks 1–4.</li>
  <li><strong>Final exam (30%)</strong> — in week 7.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Getting set up</h2>
<p>The code in the notes is plain Python 3 with only the standard library, so any recent Python installation — or a
  free <a href="https://colab.research.google.com/" rel="noopener">Google Colab</a> notebook in the browser — runs every
  example. Type the examples yourself and change them: the fastest way to understand a running-time claim is to test it.</p>
